---
// /use-cases/multi-account (2026-07-03, S14 / D8) — persona page for
// operators + account teams. Band A: the problem in their words (bans
// cascade through a shared device fingerprint), then how Driftstack
// answers it, the plan pointer, and the FAQ groups they actually read.
// Claim register: homepage §4 card ("Run many accounts, safely apart" /
// persistent profiles / own physical phone), §5 cockpit, §9 egress-live
// chip framing, pricing Manual card (10 to 200 profiles, browser tabs).
// AUP discipline: capability description only — the page names the
// boundary (/legal/aup) instead of encouraging anyone to cross it.
// 2026-09-15 truth pass (product-truth sheets): fingerprint claims scoped
// to "checked against real iPhones" (81/96 device profiles byte-identical,
// not a blanket identity); human motion is a per-profile MODE, not every
// interaction; clock + time zone follow the exit (language is not claimed);
// the free tier's device entitlement is data-bound from pricing.ts.
// 2026-09-16 readability pass: pure Band A. Short sentences, benefit first,
// "fingerprint" introduced once and linked to /glossary#fingerprint; no term
// left undefined in the sentence that uses it. Facts, numbers and the AUP
// boundary unchanged.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Stat from '../../components/Stat.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { FAQ_GROUPS } from '../../data/faq';
import { API_TIERS } from '../../data/pricing';

const freeTier = API_TIERS.find((t) => t.id === 'free')!;

// The two FAQ groups operators ask about before signing: whether the
// iPhones are real, and where the acceptable-use line sits.
const faqGroups = FAQ_GROUPS.filter((g) =>
  ['Architecture + sessions', 'Acceptable use'].includes(g.title),
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Multi-account operations"
  description="Run many accounts, safely apart. Each account gets its own saved phone: its own logins, its own history, its own connection. You manage them all from one desktop app."
  ogImage="/og/use-cases-light.png"
>
  <PageHero
    label="Use cases · Operators + account teams"
    title="Run many accounts, safely apart."
    lead="You run more than one account because the work demands it: client accounts at an agency, regional storefronts, seller profiles. The hard part isn't running them. It's keeping them apart."
  >
    <Breadcrumbs
      slot="crumbs"
      class="mb-5"
      items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Use cases', href: '/use-cases/' }, { name: 'Multi-account operations' }]}
    />
  </PageHero>

  <Section
    label="The problem"
    title="One shared device links every account you own."
  >
    <div class="mt-8 max-w-prose space-y-5 text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      <p>
        Most services don't link accounts by name or email. They link them by
        device. Every browser gives away a
        <a href="/glossary/#fingerprint" class="accent-link">fingerprint</a>:
        what a website can
        measure about a visitor. Screen size, fonts, small graphics quirks —
        dozens of little signals that add up to
        <em>"this is the same machine again."</em>
        Sign into ten accounts from one browser and the service quietly knows
        those ten accounts share a device.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's why bans cascade. One account trips a rule and the device gets
        flagged. Every account that ever touched that device can go down with
        it, including the ones that never put a foot wrong. Separate Chrome
        profiles, incognito windows, a spreadsheet of logins: none of it
        changes what the site can measure.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="How Driftstack answers it"
    title="One account, one phone."
    lead="A Driftstack profile is a saved identity. It lets each account look like its own physical phone, every session, every time."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <Stat
        plain="Each account gets its own phone"
        technical="Persistent profiles: separate cookies, storage, history, and device identity per profile"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="It holds up when a site looks closely"
        technical="The hidden checks websites run to spot fakes get the answers a real iPhone gives — checked against real devices, check by check — so a profile blends in instead of standing out"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Its own corner of the world"
        technical="Bring your own proxy or VPN, attached per profile — the phone's clock and time zone follow that location, so nothing contradicts the address a website sees"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="It can even move like a person"
        technical="Taps, swipes and keystrokes can follow patterns taken from real human movement — available per profile, with a motion signature that stays consistent across that profile's sessions"
      />
    </div>
    {/* S31 2026-07-07 (truth audit) — "open at once" is the site's
        own metaphor for CONCURRENT sessions, and manual tiers cap at
        1/3/8 concurrent; 10–200 is the stored-PROFILE cap. Reworded so
        the numbers describe what they actually are. */}
    <p class="mt-10 max-w-prose text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      The desktop app is built for exactly this workday. You keep 10 to 200
      logged-in profiles saved and ready. Open the ones you need as live
      sessions and switch between them like browser tabs. Your plan sets
      how many run at the same time. Organize your profiles in folders,
      tag them by client, and launch with one click. Pop any account out
      into its own floating device window. No code anywhere in the loop.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-prose text-base leading-7 text-tk-ink-2">
      Everyday housekeeping is built in. You can
      recover one you deleted from the recycle bin, or export
      a profile and import it again. You can also invite teammates as admins who can
      run your accounts under your plan, or as read-only members who can
      view them but not change anything. Sign-ins, key changes, session
      starts and stops, profile creates and deletes, and team changes land
      in an audit log you can export.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="Which plan"
    title="This is what the Manual plan family is for."
    lead="A person clicking → Manual. It's priced on one number: how many sessions you keep open at the same time."
  >
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
      <a href="/pricing/#manual" class="btn-secondary">See Manual pricing →</a>
      <p class="text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
        Start free first: one profile, 20-minute sessions, on the
        {freeTier.archetypeAccess}, no card required. The AI agent lets you
        describe a task in plain English and watch it run. It is on Team and
        Agency.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8 max-w-prose text-sm leading-6 text-tk-ink-3">
      One honest line before you build on us. Driftstack keeps accounts apart;
      staying inside each service's rules stays your call. Our own rules are
      written in plain words in the
      <a href="/legal/aup/" class="accent-link">Acceptable Use Policy</a>.
      Read it before you sign up if you're unsure.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    label="Questions operators ask"
    title="Before you ask — asked and answered."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 space-y-12">
      {
        faqGroups.map((group) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">{group.title}</h3>
            <FaqList entries={group.entries} class="mt-5" />
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-tk-ink-3">
      Everything else is on the
      <a href="/faq/" class="accent-link">full FAQ</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Keep them apart. Keep them alive."
    lead="Open a real iPhone session on the free tier. Watch one profile keep its logins and history across runs. No card required."
    primaryHref="/pricing/#free"
    primaryLabel="Start free"
    secondaryHref="/pricing/#manual"
    secondaryLabel="See Manual pricing"
  />
</BaseLayout>
